            )","fix_hint":"go back to count=exc.error_count(), or log the field locations only (e['loc'] for e in exc.errors()) without input values","why_it_matters":"It moves away from the sanitization rule used everywhere else and puts arbitrary upstream response fragments into the log stream and UI log viewer. Only 2 of the 3 established sites use this exact form, so the convention is slightly less firmly established than in arch-001.","silenced_marker_nearby":false}],"agent_notes":["The likely reason for this change is better diagnostics: a bare status code, or 'N validation error(s)', is hard to debug. If so, the consistent route is to expand the shared sanitizer, for example by adding the URL path without query or host, or the error locations from ValidationError.errors(). Logging the raw exception does not fit that pattern.","Importing `_sanitize_exc` from triggarr/search/engine.py into triggarr/clients/base.py would create an import cycle, because engine.py imports clients.lidarr, clients.radarr and clients.sonarr, which import clients.base. A shared helper should live under triggarr/clients/ (or a neutral utility module) and be imported by engine.py. That would also merge the inline copies at base.py:71 and tracking.py:70, which are near-duplicates of each other and of `_sanitize_exc`.","The API key goes in the X-Api-Key header (base.py:32), not a query parameter, so str(HTTPStatusError) will not usually contain it. The remaining leak paths are a user-supplied base_url carrying userinfo or proxy tokens, and query params from extra_params. A redacting sink match also only works on exact strings. The security agent should judge the actual exposure.","No new coupling, dependency, cycle or abstraction breach is introduced. The diff is limited to message formatting in one method. Subclasses (lidarr/radarr/sonarr) and callers (startup.py, web/routes.py) only see the boolean return, which is unchanged.","No test in tests/test_clients.py asserts on these log message strings, so the change will not break existing tests, and nothing tests the sanitization either."]}

